// The `prelude.poll` sub-module: a DURABLE SUBSCRIPTION over a source that can only be ASKED. Where
// `time.watch` subscribes to a SCHEDULE (the occurrences are the events) and `region.watch` subscribes
// to a NURSERY (the fibers push their escalations up), `poll.subscribe` subscribes to a source that
// pushes nothing: it ticks on a schedule, asks the caller's `fetch` what the source holds now, and
// hands each item it has not seen before to the caller's `deliver_to`. Everything hard about that
// sentence lives in "has not seen before", and this module is that one sentence written once.
//
// DELIVERY IS AT-LEAST-ONCE. That is the whole contract, and it is deliberately the WEAKER one: an
// item is delivered one or more times, never zero. A crash between a delivery and its commit
// re-delivers exactly that item; nothing is dropped. EXACTLY-ONCE IS BUILT AT THE DESTINATION, by
// making the delivery idempotent (post keyed by the item's id, upsert rather than insert, label a
// message rather than reply to it) — never by strengthening the loop, which cannot be strengthened
// without a transaction the source does not offer. Every watch in the ecosystem should state its
// guarantee in these words; a source-side "exactly-once" only ever means "exactly once per key, per
// activation", which is this module's at-least-once plus an idempotent key.
//
// WHY THIS IS A MODULE AND NOT A HELPER IN EACH PACKAGE. Two packages (gmail, google_calendar) grew
// this loop independently, ~120 lines each, and each learned the SAME four traps the hard way. The
// knowledge died in their comments, and every next source — GitHub, RSS, Notion, a database change
// feed — was going to pay for it again. The four traps are now INVARIANTS OF THE SKELETON, not advice:
//
//   1. THE CURSOR MOVES ON THE SOURCE'S CLOCK, NEVER ON THE POLLER'S. A tick that finds nothing does
//      NOT advance the cursor. The wall clock is read exactly once in a subscription's life — at
//      priming, to decide where "already there" ends (`initial`) — and never again. Advancing on the
//      poller's clock instead looks harmless and silently steps over every item that was in flight
//      while the tick ran.
//   2. THE CURSOR NEVER PASSES THE TICK THAT OBSERVED IT. An item whose mark lies in the FUTURE (an
//      imported mail carrying its own date, a row with a clock-skewed timestamp) would otherwise park
//      the cursor there and mute the subscription until that instant arrived. Capping the ADVANCE can
//      only hold the cursor BACK, which costs a re-listing that the remembered keys absorb — while the
//      KEY keeps the item's true mark, so a future-dated item stays remembered for as long as the
//      cursor sits below it and is never delivered twice.
//   3. THE COMMIT IS PER ITEM, NOT PER TICK. Deliver one item, then move the cursor; repeat. A tick
//      that fails halfway leaves the rest of its batch still ABOVE the cursor, so the next tick lists
//      them again. Committing the whole batch at the end would be exactly-once on the happy path and
//      LOSSY on the failing one, which is the wrong trade for a subscription.
//   4. A BOUNDED MEMORY FORGETS BY AGE, NEVER BY ABSENCE. A dedup memory pruned to "what this tick
//      listed" forgets a key the moment its item is missing from one listing, and re-notifies the
//      moment it comes back — a transient blip at the source is enough to deliver twice. `watermark`
//      forgets what has fallen below the floor (which can no longer be listed at all); `seen_set`
//      forgets the oldest key past its cap. Both forget by age alone.
//
// THE DEDUP REGIME IS A VALUE, NOT A COPY OF THE LOOP. The two regimes that exist in the wild are the
// two constructors of `dedup`, and the skeleton dispatches on them:
//
//   - `watermark(overlap)` — the source has a MONOTONE RECEIVE CLOCK and can be listed from an
//     instant ("mail that arrived after T", "issues updated since T"). The memory is a floor plus the
//     keys sitting at or above it, so it is CONSTANT-SIZE however long the subscription runs.
//   - `seen_set(capacity)` — the source has NO usable receive clock, only a window that slides
//     ("meetings starting in the next 30 minutes"). The memory is the last @capacity@ keys.
//
// Both ride the same loop, the same per-item commit, the same priming, the same durable cursor. What
// differs is one `match` in `advance` and one in `poll_since`.
//
// WHERE THE CURSOR LIVES IS THE CALLER'S CHOICE, AND IT IS NOT A DETAIL. A subscription whose cursor
// is only a handler `var` is re-primed every time its fiber is re-forked — and a process restart
// re-forks every fiber. The subscription then comes back UP, reports itself healthy, and silently
// skips everything that arrived while the process was down: the failure is invisible, permanent, and
// only shows up as "we never heard about that one". `continuity` makes that a decision in the source:
// `resume(key = ...)` keeps the cursor in the store (a restart resumes; the downtime is delivered),
// `fresh()` keeps it in the run alone (a restart re-primes; the downtime is skipped). There is no
// default, on purpose — the choice is too consequential to inherit.
//
// NO RETRY IS BUILT IN, exactly as in `time.watch`: a `fetch` or a `deliver_to` that throws kills the
// subscription, and resilience is COMPOSED at the call site (`supervise.forever` plus a converter that
// chooses which failures are transient). A subscription with `resume` recovers what it missed while
// the replay backed off, because the cursor outlived the failed activation — which is the composition
// this module exists to make ordinary.
//
// `poll_since` / `classify` / `commit` / `mark_primed` (and `initial` / `load` / `persist` /
// `advance`) are internal machinery: a caller never performs or handles them. They are module members
// only because Katari has no module-private declarations.

// ── the item contract: what a source must be able to say about each of its items ──────────────────

@"One item as the subscription sees it: an identity, a position, and the payload. A source becomes
subscribable by answering these three things per item — the whole adapter a package writes.
@key@ must be STABLE (the same item yields the same key on every listing) and UNIQUE among the items
that can be in the memory at once; when identity alone is not enough (a recurring meeting whose
occurrences share an id, an item that may legitimately re-fire when it moves), join the discriminator
into the key (`id ++ \" \" ++ start`). @mark@ is the item's own position in the SOURCE's receive clock,
as EPOCH MILLISECONDS — the only quantity allowed to move a `watermark` cursor, and the reason a slow
tick or a skewed poller can never step over an item. Under `seen_set` no cursor moves, so @mark@ is
unused there; record the tick instant and be done."
data item[T](
  @"The item's stable, unique dedup identity." key: string,
  @"The item's own arrival instant in the SOURCE's clock, epoch milliseconds (unused under `seen_set`)." mark: number,
  @"The payload handed to `deliver_to`." value: T,
)

// ── dedup: the memory regime, as a first-class value ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@"Dedup by a MONOTONE FLOOR in the source's own receive clock — for a source that can be listed from
an instant (\"mail that arrived after T\"). The cursor is the floor; it advances to each delivered
item's own @mark@, never past the tick that observed it, and never on the poller's clock. The memory
alongside it is only the keys at or above the floor, so it stays CONSTANT-SIZE forever: a key below
the floor has nothing left to prevent, because an item that far back can no longer be listed.

@overlap@ is how far BELOW the floor each fetch re-lists, in the same milliseconds: the slack that
covers a boundary the source rounds or reads exclusively (Gmail's `after:` takes whole SECONDS and
does not document whether it means `>` or `>=`, so `overlap = 1000` makes both readings safe). The
overlap costs no duplicates — the keys inside it are exactly the ones still remembered — and an
@overlap@ of 0 is right for a source whose listing boundary is exact."
data watermark(@"How far below the floor each fetch re-lists, in milliseconds; the keys inside it stay remembered." overlap: number)

@"Dedup by a BOUNDED SET OF KEYS — for a source with no usable receive clock, only a window that
slides (\"meetings starting within the next 30 minutes\"). No cursor moves; every fetch sees the same
`since` and works from the tick instant instead. The memory is the most recent @capacity@ keys.

A CAP IS NOT A PRUNE TO THE CURRENT WINDOW — that is trap 4, and it is why this is a cap. THE
RE-NOTIFY HAZARD IT LEAVES: forgetting the oldest key means an item that is STILL inside the window
after @capacity@ other items have been delivered will be delivered again. Size @capacity@ well above
the largest number of items one window can hold (google_calendar uses 200 for a 30-minute lead) and
the drop only ever reaches keys long past; size it too small and a busy window re-notifies its own
head. There is no cheaper bound — the memory has to forget something, and age is the only safe axis.

A SEEN-SET SUBSCRIPTION CANNOT BACKFILL, whatever its `continuity`: with no floor there is nothing to
list from, so whatever crossed the window while the subscription was down is simply gone. `resume`
still earns its keep here — it stops the re-notification storm a re-primed memory would cause — but
only a `watermark` subscription delivers its downtime."
data seen_set(@"How many keys to remember; must exceed the largest number of items one window can hold." capacity: integer)

// Which memory a subscription keeps. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type dedup = watermark | seen_set

// ── backlog: what the FIRST activation does with what the source already holds ────────────────────

@"Skip whatever the source already holds when the subscription first starts: report ARRIVALS, not
matches. Under `watermark` the cursor is primed at the starting instant, so the backlog is never even
listed; under `seen_set` the first tick RECORDS its window without delivering (the priming tick), and
delivery begins with the tick after it. This is the right shape for \"tell me about new things\" and
the WRONG one for \"tell me about everything that matches\" — a subscription is a subscription to a
transition, and a listing agent is what answers \"what is there now\"."
data skip_backlog()

@"Deliver whatever the source already holds when the subscription first starts, then keep going. Under
`watermark` the cursor starts at the epoch, so the first tick lists the source's whole history — which
is what a backfill wants and what a busy source will drown in; bound it inside the `fetch` if that
matters. Under `seen_set` there is no priming tick: the first window is delivered."
data deliver_backlog()

// What the FIRST activation does with the backlog. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type backlog = skip_backlog | deliver_backlog

// ── continuity: where the cursor lives — the restart decision, made in source ─────────────────────

@"Keep the cursor in the STORE, at @key@ — so it outlives the run that wrote it. Every commit writes
it; a fresh activation (a restart, a re-fork, a `supervise` re-run) reads it back and RESUMES. Under
`watermark` that means the items which arrived while the process was down are DELIVERED rather than
skipped; under `seen_set`, which has no floor to list from, it means the window is not re-notified
from scratch. @key@ is an ordinary store key: it resolves against the surrounding `store.workspace`
exactly like any other, so WHERE the cursor lands is the calling workspace's
decision. Give every subscription its own key — two subscriptions sharing one key overwrite each
other's memory. This is the choice to make unless the subscription genuinely wants to forget."
data resume(@"The store key the cursor record lives at, relative to the current workspace." key: string)

@"Keep the cursor in the RUN ONLY (a handler `var`, durable across a crash-recovery restart of that
same run, but gone when the run ends). Every fresh activation re-primes, so whatever arrived between
activations is skipped for good and silently. Choose it when re-priming is the semantics — a
subscription whose backlog is worthless, a probe, a test — and never merely because there is no
obvious key to name."
data fresh()

// Where the cursor lives between activations. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type continuity = resume | fresh

// ── the durable memory and the per-item verdict (internal machinery) ──────────────────────────────

@"The subscription is still recording its first tick: items are remembered, not delivered. Internal to
`subscribe`."
data priming()

@"The subscription is delivering. Internal to `subscribe`."
data live()

// Whether a tick delivers or merely records. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type phase = priming | live

@"One remembered delivery: the item's @key@, and the @mark@ that decides when the memory may forget
it. Internal to `subscribe`."
data delivered(@"The delivered item's dedup key." key: string, @"The delivered item's mark, in the source's clock." mark: number)

@"A subscription's whole memory, in one value — the thing `resume` writes to the store, so it is also
the thing an operator reads in the console when a subscription looks stuck. Internal to `subscribe`;
an app never builds one."
data cursor(
  @"The floor in the source's clock; 0 under `seen_set`, which has no floor." floor: number,
  @"The keys remembered at or above the floor (or the last `capacity` keys under `seen_set`)." recent: array[delivered],
  @"Whether the first tick has finished recording." phase: phase,
)

@"Unseen and the subscription is live: deliver it, then commit. Internal to `subscribe`."
data deliver()

@"Unseen but the subscription is still priming: commit WITHOUT delivering. Internal to `subscribe`."
data remember()

@"Already remembered: do nothing. Internal to `subscribe`."
data skip()

// One item's fate this tick. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type verdict = deliver | remember | skip

@"The instant this tick's fetch lists from: the floor minus the regime's overlap, clamped at 0.
Handled inside `subscribe`; an app does not handle it."
request poll_since() -> number

@"One item's fate, decided against the memory and the phase — the ONE place \"have I seen this\" is
asked, so no code re-tests the set. Handled inside `subscribe`; an app does not handle it."
request classify(@"The item's dedup key." key: string) -> verdict

@"Fold one item into the memory and make it durable — the PER-ITEM COMMIT (trap 3). Performed only
AFTER its delivery returns, so a failed delivery leaves the item uncommitted and the next tick lists
it again. Handled inside `subscribe`; an app does not handle it."
request commit(
  @"The item's dedup key." key: string,
  @"The item's mark, in the source's clock." mark: number,
  @"The tick's scheduled instant — the ceiling this commit may not move the cursor past." tick: number,
) -> null

@"End the priming tick: from here on an unseen item is delivered rather than merely recorded.
Performed once, at the END of the first tick, so a crash partway through priming resumes as a priming
tick rather than delivering the part it never reached. Handled inside `subscribe`; an app does not
handle it."
request mark_primed() -> null

@"The memory a subscription starts from when nothing is stored. THE ONE READ OF THE POLLER'S CLOCK in
a subscription's whole life (trap 1), and only in the one case that needs it: a `watermark` that skips
its backlog, where the starting instant is precisely the line between \"already there\" and \"arrived\".
Every other combination starts at the epoch — a `seen_set` has no floor to raise, so it skips its
backlog with a priming TICK instead. Internal to `subscribe`."
agent initial(@"The dedup regime." dedup: dedup, @"What to do with the backlog." backlog: backlog) -> cursor with io {
  match (backlog) {
    case deliver_backlog(_) -> cursor(floor = 0, recent = [], phase = live())
    case skip_backlog(_) -> {
      match (dedup) {
        // The floor starts at NOW: everything the source already holds sits below it and is never
        // listed. This clock read decides where "already there" ends and nothing else.
        case watermark(overlap => _) -> cursor(floor = time.now(), recent = [], phase = live())
        // No floor to raise, so the backlog is skipped by RECORDING the first tick instead.
        case seen_set(capacity => _) -> cursor(floor = 0, recent = [], phase = priming())
      }
    }
  }
}

@"The memory this activation starts from: the stored one under `resume` (the restart resumes), a fresh
one otherwise. A stored value that does not conform — a key overwritten by something else, a record
written by an older shape — degrades to a fresh memory rather than throwing, because a subscription
that refuses to start is strictly worse than one that re-primes. Internal to `subscribe`."
agent load(
  @"The dedup regime." dedup: dedup,
  @"What to do with the backlog." backlog: backlog,
  @"Where the cursor lives." continuity: continuity,
) -> cursor with io | store.get {
  match (continuity) {
    case fresh(_) -> initial(dedup = dedup, backlog = backlog)
    case resume(key => key) -> {
      match (store.get(key = key)) {
        case store.absent(key => _) -> initial(dedup = dedup, backlog = backlog)
        case store.found(value => value) -> {
          match (json.try_validate[cursor](value = value)) {
            case null -> initial(dedup = dedup, backlog = backlog)
            case restored -> restored
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

@"Make the memory durable, if the caller asked for a durable one. Called on every commit, so the
store write is the commit — under `fresh` it is a no-op and the handler `var` is the whole memory.
Internal to `subscribe`."
agent persist(@"Where the cursor lives." continuity: continuity, @"The memory to write." memory: cursor) -> null with store.set {
  match (continuity) {
    case fresh(_) -> null
    case resume(key => key) -> store.set(key = key, value = memory)
  }
}

@"The memory with one delivered item folded in — where the two regimes actually differ, and where
traps 1, 2 and 4 are enforced. Internal to `subscribe`."
agent advance(
  @"The dedup regime." dedup: dedup,
  @"The memory before this delivery." memory: cursor,
  @"The delivered item's key." key: string,
  @"The delivered item's mark, in the source's clock." mark: number,
  @"The tick's scheduled instant." tick: number,
) -> cursor {
  match (dedup) {
    case watermark(overlap => overlap) -> {
      // The floor moves on the ITEM's mark (trap 1) and only FORWARD, because a listing is rarely
      // ordered exactly by the receive clock; and never past the tick that observed it (trap 2), so a
      // future-dated item cannot park the cursor ahead and mute the subscription. Capping can only
      // hold the cursor back, which costs a re-listing the remembered keys absorb.
      let floor = math.max(left = memory.floor, right = math.min(left = mark, right = tick))
      // Forget by AGE alone (trap 4): a key below the new fetch boundary can no longer be listed, so
      // it has nothing left to prevent. Everything inside the overlap stays — that is what makes the
      // overlap free. The key keeps the item's TRUE mark, so a future-dated item stays remembered for
      // as long as the cursor sits below it.
      let kept = array.flatten(target = for (let entry in memory.recent) {
        next if (entry.mark >= floor - overlap) { [entry] } else { [] }
      })
      cursor(
        floor = floor,
        recent = array.append(target = kept, value = delivered(key = key, mark = mark)),
        phase = memory.phase,
      )
    }
    case seen_set(capacity => capacity) -> {
      // No floor to move; the memory is a bounded queue, and the cap is what bounds it (trap 4). See
      // `seen_set`'s own docs for the re-notify hazard the cap leaves.
      let grown = array.append(target = memory.recent, value = delivered(key = key, mark = mark))
      let total = array.length(target = grown)
      cursor(
        floor = memory.floor,
        recent = array.slice(target = grown, start = math.max(left = 0, right = total - capacity), end = total),
        phase = memory.phase,
      )
    }
  }
}

// ── subscribe: the skeleton ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"Poll a source on @schedule@ and deliver each item it has not seen before to @deliver_to@, forever —
the durable-subscription skeleton every source package composes instead of rewriting. Shaped like
`time.watch` (`-> never`, @deliver_to@'s effects @E@ flow to the caller's handlers unchanged), so it
composes under `parallel [ … ]` beside any other daemon and beside other subscriptions.

DELIVERY IS AT-LEAST-ONCE. An item reaches @deliver_to@ one or more times, never zero: the commit lands
only after a delivery returns, so a crash or a throw in between re-delivers exactly that item on the
next tick. Build exactly-once at the DESTINATION by making the delivery idempotent — keyed by
`item.key`, which is the identity this loop already promises is stable.

WHAT @fetch@ MUST GUARANTEE, and it is the whole adapter: given @since@ (the instant this tick lists
from — the cursor's floor minus the regime's overlap) and @tick@ (the occurrence's scheduled instant),
return every item at or above @since@, OLDEST FIRST, across every page of the source's pagination.
Oldest-first is load-bearing: the cursor then only ever passes items that have actually been
delivered, so a delivery that throws mid-tick leaves the rest of the tick still above the cursor.
Draining every page is load-bearing too: a listing cut off at a cap that orders newest-first hands
back only the newest matches, and a cursor advanced over them strands the older ones below it forever.
Returning an item AGAIN that was returned before is free — that is what the remembered keys are for.

WHAT IT REPORTS. With `skip_backlog` it reports ARRIVALS: whatever the source already held when the
subscription first started is never delivered, however well it matches, and an old item that comes to
match LATER is not delivered either (the fetch never looks below the cursor). That is the right shape
for hearing about new things and the wrong one for \"tell me whenever anything matches\"; a listing
agent answers the latter.

RESTARTS. With `continuity = resume(key = ...)` the cursor lives in the store, so a runtime restart, a
re-forked fiber and a `supervise` re-run all RESUME from it — and the items that arrived while the
subscription was down are delivered, oldest first, on the tick after it comes back. With `fresh()`
every activation re-primes and that downtime is skipped for good. Nothing else about the subscription
changes between the two.

NO RETRY IS BUILT IN — a failing @fetch@ or @deliver_to@ propagates and kills the subscription, exactly
as an uncaught failure in any callee does. Compose resilience at the call site: `supervise.forever` plus a
converter that turns the transient failures (and, for an OAuth source, `auth_error`) into
`supervise.interrupted`. With `resume`, the backoff costs nothing but latency; the cursor survives it.

The first poll is one @schedule@ occurrence in (a `time.watch` occurrence fires one interval AFTER the
start), and under `seen_set` + `skip_backlog` that first poll is the priming one — so the earliest
notification such a subscription can produce is two occurrences after it starts."
agent subscribe[T, effect E](
  @"When each poll fires: a `time.interval(...)` or a `time.cron(...)`." schedule: time.schedule,
  @"The dedup regime: `watermark(overlap = ...)` or `seen_set(capacity = ...)`." dedup: dedup,
  @"What the first activation does with what the source already holds." backlog: backlog,
  @"Where the cursor lives between activations — `resume(key = ...)` to survive a restart, `fresh()` not to." continuity: continuity,
  @"List the source from @since@ as of @tick@: every item at or above @since@, OLDEST FIRST, every page." fetch: agent (since: number, tick: number) -> array[item[T]] with E,
  @"Called once per item not seen before; its effects flow to the caller's handlers unchanged." deliver_to: agent (value: T) -> null with E,
) -> never with E | io | store.get | store.set {
  // The memory is a handler `var` — ONE owner, advanced only by `commit` / `mark_primed` — seeded from
  // the store so a fresh activation resumes rather than re-primes. `time.watch` owns the loop, so the
  // durable footprint stays flat however long the subscription runs: no growing recursion, no history.
  use handler (var memory: cursor = load(dedup = dedup, backlog = backlog, continuity = continuity)) {
    request poll_since() {
      next match (dedup) {
        // The overlap is subtracted HERE, once, so the fetch and the memory's pruning boundary can
        // never drift apart: what the fetch re-lists is exactly what the memory still remembers.
        case watermark(overlap => overlap) -> math.max(left = 0, right = memory.floor - overlap)
        case seen_set(capacity => _) -> memory.floor
      }
    }
    request classify(key: string) {
      let seen = for (let entry in memory.recent) { next entry.key }
      next match (array.index_of(target = seen, value = key)) {
        case null -> {
          match (memory.phase) {
            case live(_) -> deliver()
            case priming(_) -> remember()
          }
        }
        case _ -> skip()
      }
    }
    request commit(key: string, mark: number, tick: number) {
      let advanced = advance(dedup = dedup, memory = memory, key = key, mark = mark, tick = tick)
      // The durable write and the var move together: after this returns, the item is committed both
      // in this run and across restarts.
      persist(continuity = continuity, memory = advanced)
      next null with { memory = advanced }
    }
    request mark_primed() {
      match (memory.phase) {
        // Already live: nothing to end, and no durable write to spend on every later tick.
        case live(_) -> { next null }
        case priming(_) -> {
          let advanced = cursor(floor = memory.floor, recent = memory.recent, phase = live())
          persist(continuity = continuity, memory = advanced)
          next null with { memory = advanced }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // The `time.watch` deliver_to: list this tick, then walk the listing one item at a time. A local
  // agent because it captures @fetch@ and @deliver_to@; its `poll_since` / `classify` / `commit` /
  // `mark_primed` are handled by the var above, while @E@ flows out to the caller. The occurrence's
  // scheduled instant does not decide what is new — the memory does — it only caps how far the cursor
  // may travel in this tick.
  agent poll_on_tick(time: number) -> null with E | poll_since | classify | commit | mark_primed {
    for (let entry in fetch(since = poll_since(), tick = time)) {
      // Deliver-then-commit, per item (trap 3). The three-way verdict is decided ONCE, in `classify`,
      // and dispatched here — no code re-tests the memory.
      match (classify(key = entry.key)) {
        case deliver(_) -> {
          deliver_to(value = entry.value)
          commit(key = entry.key, mark = entry.mark, tick = time)
        }
        case remember(_) -> commit(key = entry.key, mark = entry.mark, tick = time)
        case skip(_) -> null
      }
    }
    // Priming ends only once the WHOLE first tick has been recorded, so a crash partway through it
    // resumes as a priming tick instead of delivering the part it never reached.
    mark_primed()
  }
  time.watch(schedule = schedule, deliver_to = poll_on_tick)
}
